# Implement — etude-9uf.1: `etude gate`

## What the implementation taught us (spec corrections)

**1. The `docs-reality` tripwire I claimed in bead .2 does not fire.** Bead .2's
runbook asserted that `scripts/docs-reality-check.sh` would force this section to
be amended once `gate` shipped. It would not have: check 3 only fires when a
stale-claim phrase and an `etude <cmd>` mention land on the **same line**, and
the runbook's phrasing ("Nothing in this section describes shipped behavior yet")
never paired them. Verified by grep after shipping the command — zero hits.

The runbook now says so in the section itself rather than quietly dropping the
claim, and names what actually keeps it honest: the obligation on the
implementing bead's verify phase to walk the contract item by item against the
built binary. A discipline, not a tripwire. I did not manufacture a keyword pair
to make the check fire — that would be gaming a check rather than having one.

**2. The adapter must emit session evidence, and I had not implemented it.** The
plan said adapters emit a `session` block; the first cut did not. Running the
real command exposed it immediately: `ResolveGateSeat` sets
`RequireSessionEvidence` for any non-deterministic seat, so
`storeSeatSessionEvidence` downgraded a perfectly good codex `go` to
`malfunction` — "agentic seat did not provide session evidence". A gate that
discards every real seat's verdict is worse than no gate. Fixed: the adapter
writes the model's raw reply as a transcript beside `$ETUDE_OUTPUT_FILE` and
references it by basename, with a content-hash session id. Pinned by a
regression in `scripts/seat-adapter_test.sh`.

This is the one that justifies the "run it for real" rule: ten green Go tests did
not catch it, because they stub the seat runner.

**3. Registry quoting was a shell artifact encoded as a contract.**
`TestMigrationCriticalSeatFieldsPreserved` asserted the codex invoke contained
`model_reasoning_effort="xhigh"` **with quotes**. An invoke is split by
`strings.Fields` before exec, so the quotes would reach codex as literal
characters. The assertion now guards the *setting* (`=xhigh`, unquoted) and
actively rejects the re-quoted form, with the reason written down. Verified
against a live codex seat, which returned a usable verdict with the unquoted
form. The test was also strengthened: every review seat must invoke through the
adapter, because a bare CLI cannot satisfy the seat contract.

## Changes

**`internal/liverun/gate_once.go` (new).** `Engine.GateStage` — resolve run →
resolve reviewed stage → derive round → compose shared prompt → run checks and
seats → synthesize → append attempt → return outcome. Reuses `runGateChecks`,
`runGateSeats`, `synthesizeVerdict`, `nextPhaseRound`, `latestStageForRole`
unchanged; this is not a second seat path. Two exported sentinels,
`ErrRunNotFound` and `ErrNoReviewableStage`, so callers can distinguish "you
haven't captured yet" from a real failure.

Writes through `runmanifest.Writer{Store}.Write` with an `ExpectedOld` CAS — the
same call `capture-gate` uses — so the two paths produce interchangeable records,
as the runbook requires.

**`internal/cli/gate.go` (new).** Flags, config loading, exit-code mapping.
`ErrGateNotPassed` carries the status. `anchorSeatCommands` resolves a
repo-relative adapter path against the repo root, because exec resolves a
relative program against the *caller's* cwd — without it, a gate run from a
subdirectory fails every seat with a "file not found" that reads as an outage.

**`scripts/seat-adapter.sh` (new).** The bridge from a model CLI to the envelope
contract. Fail-closed; the only path writing `{"verdict":"go"}` is a reply that literally
said `VERDICT: GO`. The first cut also accepted `PASS` and
`PASS_WITH_FOLLOWUPS` as `go` — the implement gate BLOCKed on that, correctly:
the prompt asks for `GO|BLOCK` and nothing else, so a near-miss token is a seat
that did not answer the question asked, and near-misses are exactly where a
lenient mapping lets an unclear answer through as a pass. Now rejected, with
regressions for `PASS`, `PASS_WITH_FOLLOWUPS` and `APPROVED`. "Last VERDICT
wins" so an echoed prompt template cannot shadow the real answer — codex does
echo it, so this is a real case, not a hypothetical.

**`scripts/seat-adapter_test.sh` (new) + `make seat-adapter-test`.** 17 checks:
verdict parsing, eight fail-closed paths (including the three near-miss tokens),
BLOCK-without-reason, session evidence, and refusal to run without the
environment etude provides.

**`.etude/registry.yaml`.** The three review seats' `invoke` now names the
adapter. Seat identities, providers, models, tiers, quorum and fallbacks are
unchanged — only the invocation becomes contract-conformant. `dev` is a stage
runner, not a review seat, and is untouched.

**`.etude/workflow.yaml`.** `env_allowlist: [HOME]` — the runner env is
`PATH` + `ETUDE_*` only, so without it every real seat fails to authenticate.
NAMES only; values are never recorded.

**Docs.** `docs/cli/etude_gate.md` generated; README usage + implemented-surface
list + example; `docs/gates.md` pointer. The supervisor runbook's contract
section amended from prescriptive to shipped, carrying correction (1).

**`internal/liverun/gate_once_test.go` (new).** Ten tests, listed in verify.

## Live proof

`etude gate` was run for real against this bead's own plan artifact, twice.

The first run (before the session-evidence fix) recorded `plan.r3`:
`opus: failed`, `codex: malfunction` ("agentic seat did not provide session
evidence") → `escalated`. That is finding (2), caught by running it.

After the fix, `plan.r4`:

```
gate plan.r4: escalated
  opus (anthropic/claude-opus): failed
    note: ... seat opus: model CLI exited non-zero
  codex (openai/gpt-5.5): go
recorded plan.r4 on refs/etude/runs/etude-9uf.1
gate did not pass; the phase must not advance
```

Both attempts are on the run ref and are left there. They are truthful records of
what the command did, and `plan.r4` is the disclosed limitation demonstrated
rather than asserted: under a Claude Code supervisor the opus seat cannot
authenticate as a subprocess, so an L2 gate escalates. Note what it did **not**
do — it did not pass on codex's lone `go`. Exit non-zero.

## Checks

`make test` green · `make lint` clean · `make seat-adapter-test` all green ·
`make docs-check` no diff · `make docs-reality` OK, 15 commands.

Negative control: loosening `GateOutcome.Passed()` to accept `rerun` fails
`TestGateStageBlockDoesNotPassAndCarriesRequired` and
`TestGateStageFailingCheckDoesNotPass`, so the pass predicate is load-bearing.

## Blast radius

`internal/liverun` gains one exported method and two sentinels; nothing existing
changed. `internal/cli` gains a command. The registry/workflow edits change how
seats are invoked for **all** consumers of that config, including `etude run` —
which is the point (a bare CLI never satisfied the seat contract), and the
migration guard test now pins it.
